From 344a35e7f46c8fa8b715d7ad9d40d29ab86d7366 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 12:21:29 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] High frame rates: even pacing on fixed screens, lost
 blanks kept, a cheaper take-down; IOP log floods collapsed

burnout3-pc #26 (stutter at 120, fine at 60):

- FRAME RATE on a fixed-refresh screen (VRR off, its refresh known) is one
  of the screen's whole fractions (refresh_lock::EvenRate): 120 on a 120 or
  240 Hz screen, the screen's own on 144 or 165 Hz, where 120 pictures could
  not be shown evenly (one in five shown twice). The blanks run at exactly
  that rate, the screen's as reported, not SetFastForward(2)'s 119.88: that
  against a 120.00 Hz screen gained a picture every 8.3 s (a stutter at a
  steady beat) wherever the refresh lock was not in force. The step down
  where the PC does not keep up goes to the screen's next fraction where
  there is one (EvenRateBelow). Scripted runs (virtual time) and VRR
  screens are unchanged.
- Blanks the timer lost (LostVblanks: more than three waiting for a game
  that is behind - 25 ms at 120) count as the picture's time in
  HighFpsSteps / HighFpsFrameSteps (at most 8 a frame): before, each was a
  step's time lost and the race went on in slow motion while the pictures
  still came at 120. They also count with late pictures in KeepUp's step
  down.
- TakeDown / Measure / PlaceBetween: the transforms' tables are sorted
  vectors walked side by side instead of hash maps (~850 allocations and
  frees a step each, three maps). Checked with a synthetic game (300 moving
  RwMatrix transforms, a camera, teleports; 2400 pictures at 120): the
  placed RAM identical after every picture, old and new; the high frame
  rate's own time a picture about 25% less (850 transforms in 2 MB).

burnout3-pc #37 (the log grew to 500 MB): a module's line repeated is
counted and said at 10, 100, 1000... times, and a failed sceCdRead is said
(the first five) with where and why.

Tests: refresh_lock's EvenRate / EvenRateBelow in host_tests.

